WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:03.830
Film Courage: In a nutshell, what are the
basic storytelling principles for a screenplay,

00:00:03.830 --> 00:00:12.140
if you had a list of bullet points? Jen Grisanti:
I would say (for me) it really is the understanding

00:00:12.140 --> 00:00:21.040
of the wound, the drive and the negative narrative
that gets in the way and how the pursuit is

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:29.100
one step toward healing the wound. I would
say that is definitely one. Another story

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:37.750
tool that I use which also goes into the Why
is how does the personal dilemma of the central

00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:48.440
character link to the professional pursuit?
And really being clear on if there is a link.

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:55.860
And very often, there isn’t a link. You
are setting up a weakness or a void that isn’t

00:00:55.860 --> 00:01:02.879
connected to the pursuit and how the pursuit
can be the answer to that void. And then I

00:01:02.879 --> 00:01:12.030
would say having an active hero who has a
clear want is definitely a huge thing that

00:01:12.030 --> 00:01:17.859
I work with writers on because it’s really
thinking about….I mean I think when I first

00:01:17.859 --> 00:01:23.340
started consulting one of the biggest questions
I asked was what does the central character

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:31.719
want? If I don’t know that, the story doesn’t
work. What I came to recognize when I wrote

00:01:31.719 --> 00:01:38.119
storyline, I recognized there were a hundred
screenwriting books out there. What’s going

00:01:38.119 --> 00:01:45.899
to make my voice different? And at that point
I had been an analyst for 17 years. So I recognized

00:01:45.899 --> 00:01:51.770
that analyzing is what sets me apart because
I know how to do that. This is what my brain

00:01:51.770 --> 00:01:58.549
has been trained for. And so I got a bunch
of Oscar nominated, Emmy nominated and Golden

00:01:58.549 --> 00:02:05.030
Globe nominated scripts and I extracted a
formula because I thought I don’t want to

00:02:05.030 --> 00:02:09.619
teach writers how to write a good script because
that won’t get them staffed and that won’t

00:02:09.619 --> 00:02:14.969
lead them to sell a script. I want to teach
them to write a script that hits it out of

00:02:14.969 --> 00:02:22.519
the ballpark. And that formula which I came
upon was that every strong story starts with

00:02:22.519 --> 00:02:29.019
a trigger incident or an inciting incident
as many people term it and it pushes the central

00:02:29.019 --> 00:02:39.040
character into a powerful dilemma. Then the
choice that is made in the dilemma is what

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:47.890
defines the external goal for a story. Every
obstacle, escalating obstacle and all is lost

00:02:47.890 --> 00:02:56.190
moments need to link back to that choice.
And when the goal isn’t clear the story

00:02:56.190 --> 00:03:06.650
doesn’t work because everything needs to
link back to, because there are countless

00:03:06.650 --> 00:03:13.430
features as well as TV shows where in the
middle of the story you have no idea what

00:03:13.430 --> 00:03:19.290
the central character wants, like you’ve
lost it. Because the actions and obstacles

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:27.150
aren’t linked to the desire and we lose
a sense as to what the desire was. So when

00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:36.660
you do that, story doesn’t work. And very
often writers will write a passive hero that

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:43.010
isn’t taking action. Another mistake writers
will make is that they will choose the wrong

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:53.349
lead. They will develop another character
more with regards to backstory and empathy

00:03:53.349 --> 00:03:59.180
than they will their lead. Film Courage: Interesting….Jen
Grisanti: So they choose the wrong lead. Film

00:03:59.180 --> 00:04:03.099
Courage: If someone is looking at their own
story, how can they tell they have a passive

00:04:03.099 --> 00:04:09.349
hero? Jen Grisanti: Are they taking action
toward the goal? It’s very easy….are they

00:04:09.349 --> 00:04:18.299
reacting to what happens to them? If you look
at act breaks….at the act break the central

00:04:18.299 --> 00:04:22.940
character should take an action toward the
goal, hit an obstacle and then there should

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:29.560
be a reminder of the stakes, that is IF the
stakes were set up well at the beginning.

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:37.250
And the internal and external stakes should
be felt at that act break. But many times

00:04:37.250 --> 00:04:45.130
the act break is something that happens to
the central character rather than it being

00:04:45.130 --> 00:04:52.440
a a result of an action they took toward the
goal that led to the obstacle. Film Courage:

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:58.590
How soon should the key desire be revealed
within the story? Jen Grisanti: That’s a

00:04:58.590 --> 00:05:04.319
good question. End of act one. We should have
a clear sense of what the central character

00:05:04.319 --> 00:05:14.900
wants by the end of act one. And I am always
studying story and figuring out how to teach

00:05:14.900 --> 00:05:21.870
in a way that will connect with the writer.
So I go off of the brilliant work being produced.

00:05:21.870 --> 00:05:30.440
I would say that I have seen the end of act
one be on the clear set-up of the goal and

00:05:30.440 --> 00:05:36.430
that works well. Or I’ve seen act one end
on the first action taken toward the goal

00:05:36.430 --> 00:05:45.620
and the first obstacle hit. So either of those
are fine. I would say probably most stories

00:05:45.620 --> 00:05:49.599
the first action toward the goal is often
in act two. Question: Do you read Oscar or

00:05:49.599 --> 00:05:49.740
Emmy nominated scripts every year?

